.TH LIKEOS-INSTALLER 1 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
likeos-installer \- LikeOS Setup, the graphical installer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B likeos-installer
.RB [ \-\-mock ]
.RB [ \-\-window ]
.RB [ \-\-page=\fIn\fP
.RB [ \-\-substep=\fIn\fP ]]
.RB [ \-\-size=\fIw\fPx\fIh\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-fast ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-animation ]
.br
.B likeos-installer \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B likeos-installer
is LikeOS Setup: the program that installs LikeOS from the USB medium onto
a disk of the computer.
It is normally not started by hand.
The boot menu of the installation medium has an entry
.BR "Install LikeOS from USB" ;
it starts the medium's system with
.B likeos.mode=install
on the kernel command line, and
.BR init (8)
then starts LikeOS Setup on the console instead of the login prompt: the
X server with this program alone, full screen, without a window manager or
desktop.
.PP
Setup asks, page by page: the language and the formats of dates and times,
the keyboard to use during setup, where LikeOS goes (a disk to erase
completely, or a partition, with tools to delete, create, format and extend
partitions \(em nothing is written until the installation starts), the time
zone, the keyboard layouts of the new system, the computer's name, the root
password and the account to sign in with.
A summary shows every choice before anything is changed; Install then
partitions and formats, unpacks the system image, sets up the new system,
installs the boot loader and registers it with the firmware, showing the
progress, and restarts the computer into the installed system.
.PP
The disks of the medium Setup was started from are shown but cannot be
chosen.
When something goes wrong a page says what, with the installation log.
.PP
The work is done by the installer library that the text-mode installer,
.BR likeos-install (1),
uses too.
When the graphical installer cannot run (the graphics of the computer do
not work), the installation medium's console offers
.B likeos-install
instead.
.SH OPTIONS
These are for developing and testing Setup; the installation medium starts
it without any.
.TP
.B \-\-mock
Use the built-in stand-in for the installer library: made-up disks and a
simulated installation that changes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-window
Run in a normal 1024\(mu768 window instead of covering the screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-page= n
Start on page
.I n
(0 the start screen, 1 language, 2 keyboard, 3 preparing, 4 disk, 5 time
zone, 6 keyboard layouts, 7 computer name, 8 root password, 9 account, 10
summary, 11 installing, 12 finished, 13 error), with made-up answers for the
pages before it \(em for screenshots.
.TP
.BI \-\-substep= n
With
.BR \-\-page :
go
.I n
steps into a page that has steps.
.TP
.BI \-\-size= w x h
Lay the pages out as if the screen were
.IR w \(mu h
pixels.
.TP
.B \-\-fast
No minimum time on the start and waiting screens.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-animation
Still illustrations and spinners.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The installation finished (Setup restarts the computer itself).
.TP
.B 10
The person chose to switch the computer off.
.TP
.B other
Setup could not run or broke down; the installation medium's console then
offers the text-mode installer.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /install/likeos-rootfs.tar.zst
The system image that is installed.
.TP
.B /tmp/likeos-install.log
The log of the installation (kept on the installed system as
.BR /var/log/likeos-install.log ).
.TP
.B /etc/inittab.install
What
.BR init (8)
runs when the medium was started with
.BR likeos.mode=install .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR likeos-install (1),
.BR likeos-install.conf (5),
.BR likeos-bootmenu.conf (5),
.BR likeos-gpt (1),
.BR likeos-efiboot (1)
